<script>
const BOOKS = [{"title": "Read Receipts", "blurb": "Naledi is removed from the group chat on a Tuesday afternoon. Nobody tells her why, and she spends eleven days working it out.", "n": 1, "pages": [["## One", "She noticed at twenty past four, which she remembers because she had been checking her phone roughly every ninety seconds for an hour and there had been nothing.", "Not nothing as in quiet. Nothing as in gone. The group was not in her list any more."], ["The group was called <i>the girls</i> and it had six people in it and it had been going for two years.", "It was where plans happened. It was where you found out that netball was cancelled, or that somebody's cousin had died, or what everybody was wearing on civvies day.", "You did not get removed from it. Nobody had ever been removed from it."], ["## Two", "Naledi's first thought was that it was a glitch. Phones do strange things.", "She restarted it. She turned the data off and on. She checked whether she had accidentally left, which you can do, apparently, by pressing something in the wrong place."], ["Her second thought, arriving about four minutes after the first, was much worse and much more likely.", "Somebody had done it on purpose."], ["## Three", "She did not message anybody that night.", "That took an enormous amount of self-control and she is still, years later, quite proud of it."], ["What she wanted to do was message all five of them individually and ask what was going on, in capital letters.", "What she understood, dimly, was that doing that would make her the person who made a fuss. And whatever had happened, being the person who made a fuss would make it worse."], ["## Four", "At school the next day nothing happened.", "That was the strange part. Amo said hello. Thandi asked to borrow a pen. They sat in the same places at break and talked about the same things."], ["Nobody mentioned the group. Nobody looked at her strangely.", "By the end of the day Naledi was starting to wonder whether she had imagined the whole thing, which she had not, because she checked her phone eleven times and the group was still gone."], ["## Five", "On the third day she noticed something."], ["At break, Amo said something to Thandi and Thandi laughed, and Naledi had no idea what they were talking about.", "That had never happened before. There had always been a thread running under everything, from the chat, that meant you knew the background of every joke."], ["Now the thread was cut and she was standing on the outside of a conversation she used to be inside."], ["## Six", "On the fifth day she asked Thandi. Carefully. In the corridor, on the way to Afrikaans, with nobody else there.", "“Is there a new group? I think I got removed from the old one.”"], ["Thandi's face did a whole set of things in about half a second.", "“I do not know,” she said. “I think it just moved. I have not really been looking.”", "Which was a lie, and both of them knew it was a lie, and Thandi looked away first."], ["## Seven", "The thing Naledi could not work out was the reason.", "She went back over everything she had said for three weeks. She reread her own messages, which is a horrible thing to do and which she does not recommend.", "She found nothing. She had been perfectly ordinary. She had been, if anything, boring."], ["## Eight", "On the eighth day Karabo told her.", "Karabo was not really in the group. She was on the edge of it, the way there is always somebody on the edge, and that turned out to be useful."], ["“It is not about you,” said Karabo. “It is about Amo and Lerato. They are fighting. Lerato made a new group without Amo in it, and Amo made a new group without Lerato in it.”", "“So why am I not in either?”", "Karabo shrugged. “Because you are friends with both of them. They both think you will tell the other one.”"], ["## Nine", "Naledi sat with that for a long time.", "She had spent eight days assuming she had done something wrong. She had gone back through three weeks of messages looking for her own crime."], ["And the actual answer was that she had been trusted by two people who had stopped trusting each other, and that had made her dangerous to both of them.", "Being liked by everybody, it turned out, was not a safe position. It was a suspicious one."], ["## Ten", "The two groups lasted about a month.", "Then Amo and Lerato made up, in the sudden complete way that fights at that age end, and a new group appeared with all six of them in it and a new name."], ["Naledi was added back without comment. Nobody mentioned it. There was no apology and she did not expect one and she has thought about that quite a lot since."], ["## Eleven", "She did one thing differently afterwards, and it was small.", "She stopped being the person who knew everybody's business."], ["When Amo told her something about Lerato she said, “I am not the right person to tell that to.” It felt rude the first four or five times.", "After that it stopped feeling rude and started feeling like relief."], ["## Twelve", "She is nineteen now and she still does it.", "She says the eleven days were the most useful eleven days of high school, which is a strange thing to say about being miserable."], ["“I thought the lesson was about them,” she says. “It was not. The lesson was that I had made myself the middle of something, and the middle is the first place people cut when they want to hurt each other.”"]], "wc": 916, "words": [{"w": "glitch", "m": "a small fault in a machine or programme"}, {"w": "restarted", "m": "started again"}, {"w": "individually", "m": "one at a time, separately"}, {"w": "corridor", "m": "a passage inside a building"}, {"w": "ordinary", "m": "normal, nothing special"}, {"w": "suspicious", "m": "making people think something is wrong"}, {"w": "apology", "m": "saying sorry"}, {"w": "relief", "m": "the good feeling when worry stops"}, {"w": "miserable", "m": "very unhappy"}, {"w": "trusted", "m": "believed to be honest"}]}, {"title": "The Interview", "blurb": "Zanele is thirteen. The job says sixteen and up. She decides that the gap is small enough to jump.", "n": 2, "pages": [["## One", "The notice was stuck to the window of the coffee place in the little shopping centre, printed on a sheet of A4 that had gone slightly curly in the sun.", "<i>Weekend help wanted. Saturdays and Sundays. 16+. Ask inside.</i>"], ["Zanele was thirteen years and four months old.", "She stood and looked at that notice for a long time, and what she was actually doing, she realised later, was negotiating with herself."], ["## Two", "The negotiation went like this.", "Sixteen was the rule. She was not sixteen. But she was tall, and people always thought she was older, and what they really meant by sixteen was <i>old enough to be reliable</i>, and she was more reliable than most sixteen-year-olds she knew."], ["So really, she told herself, the number was a way of asking a question, and she had the right answer to the question even though she had the wrong number."], ["## Three", "She went in on the Thursday.", "The woman behind the counter was called Ruth and she had a pen behind her ear and she was doing four things at once."], ["“I saw the notice,” said Zanele. “About the weekend help.”", "Ruth looked up properly. “How old are you?”", "And Zanele, who had rehearsed this in the mirror, said, “Sixteen.”"], ["## Four", "It came out easily, which frightened her afterwards more than anything else that happened.", "She had expected it to stick. She had expected to go red. Instead it came out as smoothly as her own name."], ["Ruth wrote something down.", "“Can you work this Saturday?”", "“Yes.”", "“Seven until two. Be early. We will see how it goes.”"], ["## Five", "She was good at it.", "That is the part of the story she still finds complicated, because it would be a tidier story if she had been terrible."], ["She was quick. She remembered orders without writing them down. She saw when a table needed clearing before anybody asked.", "By the third Saturday Ruth was leaving her alone at the till for ten minutes at a time, which is not a small thing."], ["## Six", "The money was three hundred rand a weekend.", "She gave a hundred to her mother, who did not ask enough questions about where it came from, and kept two hundred in a shoe."], ["By the sixth weekend there was one thousand two hundred rand in the shoe.", "She had never had more than about eighty rand in her life."], ["## Seven", "It came apart on a Sunday in the seventh week, and not in the way she expected."], ["A woman came in with two children and ordered, and while Zanele was making the order the woman said, quite pleasantly, “You are in my daughter's year, are you not? At Rosewood? Grade 7?”", "There was a silence that Zanele can still hear."], ["## Eight", "She said, “No, I think you have the wrong person,” and finished the order, and her hands did not shake until afterwards.", "The woman was not sure. She frowned a bit and took her coffee and sat down.", "But Ruth had been standing two metres away, wiping the machine, and Ruth had heard all of it."], ["## Nine", "Ruth waited until the end of the shift.", "She waited until the shop was empty and the door was locked and the chairs were up, which Zanele understood later was a kindness."], ["“How old are you actually?”", "Zanele thought about lying again. She could feel the lie sitting there, ready.", "“Thirteen,” she said."], ["## Ten", "Ruth put down the cloth and leaned against the counter and did not say anything for a while."], ["“I cannot employ you,” she said eventually. “Not because you are bad at it. You are the best weekend person I have had in two years. Because it is against the law and it is my licence and my whole business, and you knew that when you said sixteen.”"], ["“I know.”", "“Do you? Because I do not think you thought about my side of it at all.”"], ["## Eleven", "That was the sentence that landed.", "Not the law. Not the lie. The fact that she had thought about the number as something standing between her and what she wanted, and never once as something standing between Ruth and losing everything."], ["She had made the whole thing about herself and not noticed she was doing it."], ["## Twelve", "Ruth paid her for the day and told her to come back when she was sixteen and that she would have a job, and she meant it.", "Zanele went back three years later. Ruth remembered her immediately and gave her the job."], ["She still has the shoe with the money in it, which she never spent.", "“I thought I was being brave,” she says now. “I was being brave with somebody else's licence.”"]], "wc": 778, "words": [{"w": "notice", "m": "a printed sign giving information"}, {"w": "negotiating", "m": "talking to reach an agreement"}, {"w": "reliable", "m": "can be depended on"}, {"w": "rehearsed", "m": "practised beforehand"}, {"w": "complicated", "m": "not simple"}, {"w": "pleasantly", "m": "in a friendly way"}, {"w": "kindness", "m": "being kind"}, {"w": "employ", "m": "to give somebody a job"}, {"w": "licence", "m": "official permission to trade"}, {"w": "eventually", "m": "in the end, after some time"}]}, {"title": "Eleven Percent", "blurb": "Ofentse runs for class representative and loses by a distance. Working out why takes her longer than the campaign did.", "n": 3, "pages": [["## One", "There were three people on the ballot and Ofentse got eleven percent of the vote.", "Eleven percent of thirty-four learners is just under four people. Four people, in a class where she had been for two and a half years."], ["She did the arithmetic in her head while Mr Sithole was reading the result out, which she regrets, because the arithmetic was very fast and the rest of the announcement took another two minutes and she had to stand there."], ["## Two", "She had run a proper campaign.", "That is what made it worse. She had not just put her hand up. She had made posters. She had written a speech and practised it. She had a list of five things she would change, with costs."], ["Katlego, who won, had stood up and spoken for forty seconds without notes and made everybody laugh twice."], ["## Three", "Her first explanation was that it was a popularity contest and that popularity contests are stupid.", "This explanation had the advantage of requiring nothing from her."], ["She held it for about a week. It was comfortable. It was also, she began to suspect, not quite true, because Boitumelo had come second with thirty-one percent, and Boitumelo was not especially popular either."], ["## Four", "So she did something that she now thinks was the most useful thing she has ever done, and which she only did because she was angry.", "She asked people."], ["Not in a wounded way. She practised the question until it sounded ordinary.", "“I am trying to work out what I got wrong. What would have made you vote for me?”"], ["## Five", "She asked eleven people over two weeks. Nine answered.", "Their answers were not what she expected and were remarkably similar to one another, which is what made them impossible to argue with."], ["Four people said some version of: <i>your list was about the class, but it sounded like it was about you.</i>", "Three said they had not really understood what a class rep does, so they voted for whoever seemed nice."], ["Two said her posters were the best but she had never actually spoken to them, personally, ever."], ["## Six", "That last one was the one that kept her awake.", "She counted it up. Of the thirty-four people in that class, she had had a real conversation with maybe twelve.", "She knew everybody's name. She had spoken to a third of them."], ["She had made posters for people she had never said hello to, and then been surprised when they did not choose her."], ["## Seven", "The five things on her list were good. She still thinks they were good.", "A working kettle in the classroom for winter. A rota for the cleaning so that it was not always the same four girls. A noticeboard for lost property."], ["But she had written them alone, at home, from what she had noticed.", "She had not asked one single person what they wanted before she promised it."], ["## Eight", "Katlego, who had won, asked her in the second week to help with the cleaning rota.", "Ofentse said yes immediately and then felt strange about saying yes immediately, and had to work out why."], ["The reason was that a small part of her had wanted him to fail, so that the eleven percent would look like the class's mistake instead of hers.", "She did not like finding that in herself."], ["## Nine", "She helped with the rota. It was her system, largely, and it worked, and it is still the system that class used the following year.", "Nobody knew it was hers. Katlego knew. Mr Sithole knew.", "Thirty-two other people thought the class rep had sorted out the cleaning."], ["## Ten", "She ran again the next year.", "This time she did almost nothing that looked like campaigning. No posters at all."], ["What she did instead, over about six weeks, was talk to every person in that class. Properly. About their own things, not about her.", "It took her a long time and some of the conversations were awkward and two of them went nowhere."], ["## Eleven", "She won with fifty-eight percent.", "Her speech was thirty seconds long and it was mostly other people's ideas with their names attached."], ["Afterwards a girl called Refiloe said, “You put my thing about the water bottles in your speech,” and looked pleased about it for the rest of the day."], ["## Twelve", "The thing Ofentse says about it now is not <i>be nice to people</i>, which she thinks is too small a lesson and slightly dishonest.", "What she says is that at thirteen she had believed the best plan wins, and that this is simply not how anything works, and that nobody had told her."], ["“The plan matters,” she says. “It matters second. Whether people think you actually see them matters first. I lost by twenty points learning that.”"]], "wc": 791, "words": [{"w": "ballot", "m": "the paper or list people vote on"}, {"w": "campaign", "m": "organised work to win support"}, {"w": "arithmetic", "m": "working with numbers"}, {"w": "popularity", "m": "being liked by many people"}, {"w": "explanation", "m": "a reason given for something"}, {"w": "remarkably", "m": "in a surprising way"}, {"w": "promised", "m": "said you would definitely do it"}, {"w": "system", "m": "an organised way of doing something"}, {"w": "awkward", "m": "uncomfortable or not easy"}, {"w": "dishonest", "m": "not truthful"}]}, {"title": "The Blazer", "blurb": "Second-hand uniform sale, Saturday morning, eight o'clock. Kgomotso's mother is in the queue by half past six.", "n": 4, "pages": [["## One", "The second-hand uniform sale happened twice a year in the school hall, and by seven in the morning the queue went out of the gate and down along the fence."], ["Kgomotso's mother had been there since half past six.", "Kgomotso knew this because she had woken up at a quarter past six and her mother had already gone."], ["## Two", "The blazer was the expensive thing.", "New, from the uniform shop, a blazer was one thousand one hundred rand. Second-hand, at the sale, a good one went for about two hundred and fifty."], ["There were never enough good ones. That was the whole reason for the queue."], ["## Three", "Her mother got one. Size 34, navy, with the badge still properly stitched and only slight shine on the elbows.", "She was extremely pleased with herself and told the story of getting it three separate times that day, including the part where a woman had tried to take it out of her hand."], ["Kgomotso said thank you and meant it, and then looked at the inside collar when her mother was not in the room."], ["## Four", "There was a name tape sewn into the collar.", "<i>C. VAN WYK.</i>"], ["Charlotte van Wyk was in her year. Charlotte van Wyk sat two rows in front of her in Natural Sciences.", "Charlotte van Wyk had grown out of this blazer in June and her mother had brought it to the sale, and now Kgomotso was going to wear it to school on Monday."], ["## Five", "She did not sleep very well that Sunday night."], ["What she kept thinking about was not the blazer. It was the moment somebody would notice.", "She could see it completely. Somebody would look at the collar, or Charlotte would recognise the mark on the sleeve, and there would be a small sound, and then everybody would know."], ["## Six", "She tried to unpick the name tape on Sunday night with her mother's small scissors.", "She made a hole. It was not a big hole but it was in the collar, where a hole shows."], ["She sat on the floor of her room at eleven o'clock at night holding a blazer with a hole in it and had what she now describes, quite calmly, as the worst hour of that entire year."], ["## Seven", "Her mother found her there.", "Kgomotso had expected shouting, because two hundred and fifty rand is two hundred and fifty rand."], ["Her mother sat down on the floor next to her, which took a while because of her knee, and looked at the hole and then at the name tape.", "“Ah,” she said. Just that."], ["## Eight", "“I did not want them to know,” said Kgomotso.", "“I know what you did not want.”"], ["“I can wear my old one. It is only a bit short.”", "“It is four centimetres short and the cuffs are through,” said her mother. “No.”"], ["## Nine", "They fixed it on Monday morning at five o'clock, which is when her mother did things that needed doing.", "She cut the name tape out completely, hole and all, and sewed a small square of navy over the gap, on the inside, where nobody would ever see it."], ["Then she sewed a new name tape over the top of that. <i>K. MOTAUNG.</i>", "It took her about forty minutes and she did it without her glasses because she could not find them."], ["## Ten", "Nobody noticed. Not that Monday, not ever.", "Charlotte van Wyk did not glance at her once."], ["Kgomotso wore that blazer for two years and grew out of it in Grade 9, and her mother took it to the sale in the June of that year, and somebody else's mother queued from half past six for it."], ["## Eleven", "She thinks now that she was afraid of the wrong thing.", "She had been afraid of being found out as the girl with the second-hand blazer. Which, she has since worked out, was about two thirds of that school."], ["The queue went out of the gate and down along the fence. It was not a small queue. She had stood in a corridor full of people wearing each other's old clothes and believed she was the only one."], ["## Twelve", "What she remembers is her mother on the floor at eleven at night, not shouting.", "And her mother at five in the morning sewing a navy square over a hole, without her glasses, so that her daughter could walk in and be nobody in particular."], ["“She understood exactly what I was afraid of,” Kgomotso says. “She did not tell me it was silly. She just quietly made it not true.”"]], "wc": 755, "words": [{"w": "uniform", "m": "the clothes everybody at a school wears"}, {"w": "queue", "m": "a line of people waiting"}, {"w": "expensive", "m": "costing a lot of money"}, {"w": "collar", "m": "the part of a shirt or jacket round the neck"}, {"w": "recognise", "m": "to know something when you see it again"}, {"w": "scissors", "m": "you cut with them"}, {"w": "describes", "m": "says what something is like"}, {"w": "centimetres", "m": "there are a hundred of these in a metre"}, {"w": "glasses", "m": "you wear them to see better"}, {"w": "corridor", "m": "a passage inside a building"}]}, {"title": "Somebody Else's Face", "blurb": "There is an account using Amahle's photographs and her name. It has four hundred followers. She did not make it.", "n": 5, "pages": [["## One", "Her cousin sent it to her on a Wednesday night with three question marks and nothing else.", "It was a screenshot of a profile. Her face. Her name, spelled correctly. Four hundred and twelve followers."], ["Amahle did not have that account. Amahle had one account with ninety-one followers, mostly family, on which she posted about twice a month."], ["## Two", "The photographs were hers.", "That was the first thing that made her feel sick. They were not stolen from strangers. They were her photographs, taken off her own page, including one of her in her school uniform outside her own front gate."], ["Which meant whoever had made it knew what she looked like, where she went to school and where she lived."], ["## Three", "The posts were not terrible, which somehow made it stranger.", "There were no cruel captions. Nothing embarrassing. It was just her, posting ordinary things, in a slightly different voice from her own."], ["Saying she was fifteen. Saying she was from a different suburb. Replying to people in the comments in a friendly, chatty way that Amahle would never have used."], ["## Four", "She sat on her bed for about an hour and did a thing that she now knows was the wrong instinct.", "She started reading all of it."], ["Every post. Every comment. Every reply. Looking for the moment that would tell her who it was.", "By midnight she had read four months of somebody pretending to be her and she felt like she had been turned inside out."], ["## Five", "She told her mother the next morning.", "That was the right thing and she nearly did not do it, because a part of her was convinced she had somehow caused it by posting photographs in the first place."], ["Her mother did not say that. Her mother said, “We are going to write down everything before we touch anything,” which turned out to be exactly right."], ["## Six", "They took screenshots of everything. The profile. The posts. The follower list. The date each post went up.", "Her mother put them all in one folder with the date on it.", "“Why?” said Amahle.", "“Because the moment you report it, it disappears. And then it is your word against nothing.”"], ["## Seven", "They reported it. It came down in four days, which was faster than Amahle expected and slower than four days usually feels."], ["Her mother also went to the school, which Amahle fought her about for an entire evening and lost.", "“They do not need to know.”", "“If it is somebody at that school, they are the only people who can find out.”"], ["## Eight", "It was somebody at that school.", "It was a girl two years below her, who Amahle had spoken to perhaps four times in her life and could not have picked out of a group."], ["She had made the account because she wanted to be somebody with four hundred followers, and being Amahle had seemed easier than being herself."], ["## Nine", "Amahle was not ready for how that made her feel, which was not anger.", "It was something closer to embarrassment on the other girl's behalf, and she has never fully been able to explain it to anybody."], ["The girl was twelve. She cried in the office. Her mother came in from work and cried too.", "Amahle sat there and wished very strongly that she was somewhere else."], ["## Ten", "Three things changed afterwards, and only one of them was about settings."], ["She made her account private, which took about eleven seconds and which she should have done years earlier.", "She stopped posting photographs in school uniform, because a uniform is a map to a building where somebody knows you will be at eight in the morning."], ["And she stopped assuming that the internet version of a person is the person."], ["## Eleven", "Her mother was right about the screenshots.", "When the school asked for evidence, the account was already gone. What they had was a folder with everything in it and a date on the front, and that folder was the whole reason anything happened at all."], ["Amahle has done that ever since, for everything. Screenshot first. Report second."], ["## Twelve", "The part she thinks about most is not the account.", "It is that for four months somebody had been walking around inside her name, talking to strangers, and she had no idea, and she only found out because a cousin happened to see it."], ["“I check my own name now,” she says. “About once a month. It takes two minutes. Everybody thinks that is strange until it happens to them.”"]], "wc": 747, "words": [{"w": "account", "m": "a personal page you sign in to"}, {"w": "profile", "m": "the page that shows who you are"}, {"w": "screenshot", "m": "a picture of what is on your screen"}, {"w": "followers", "m": "people who see what you post"}, {"w": "captions", "m": "the words written under a picture"}, {"w": "suburb", "m": "an area of a town where people live"}, {"w": "instinct", "m": "a feeling about what to do, without thinking"}, {"w": "evidence", "m": "proof that something happened"}, {"w": "private", "m": "not open to everybody"}, {"w": "embarrassment", "m": "the hot feeling of being ashamed"}]}, {"title": "The Long Way Round", "blurb": "For six weeks Thabang walks home a way that takes eighteen minutes longer. His sister works out why before he tells anybody.", "n": 6, "pages": [["## One", "The short way home was down Mokoena Street, past the spaza, over the open ground and in at the back gate. Eleven minutes.", "The long way was up to the main road, along past the garage, down the hill and round. Twenty-nine minutes."], ["From the second week of term, Thabang went the long way every single day."], ["## Two", "Nobody noticed for a while, because nobody counts how long somebody else takes to walk home.", "His sister Palesa noticed in the fourth week, and only because she needed him for something and he was not there."], ["“Where were you?”", "“Walking.”", "“For half an hour?”", "“It is not half an hour,” said Thabang, which was a mistake, because it told her he knew exactly how long it was."], ["## Three", "She did not ask again. She watched.", "That is the thing about an older sister. She had four days of watching before he had any idea she was doing it."], ["On the Thursday she walked out of the gate at the same time he did and went the short way and stood at the corner of the open ground, where you can see both routes."], ["## Four", "There were four boys on the open ground.", "They were not doing anything. They were sitting on the concrete pipes that had been there for two years, and they were fifteen or sixteen, and they were the reason."], ["Palesa watched Thabang come out of the school gate, look down Mokoena Street for about two seconds, and turn up towards the main road."], ["## Five", "She did not say anything that night.", "She thought about it for a day first, which she says now was the single most useful thing she did."], ["Because her first instinct had been to go down to the open ground and shout at four sixteen-year-old boys, and her second thought was that this would end with her brother having to walk past them tomorrow and the day after and every day for three years."], ["## Six", "She asked him on the Saturday, when they were washing dishes and not looking at each other, which is when people say things."], ["“The boys on the pipes.”", "Thabang did not answer for a long time.", "“They have not done anything,” he said eventually.", "“I did not ask if they had done anything. I asked about the boys on the pipes.”"], ["## Seven", "It came out slowly and not in order, which is how these things come out.", "They had not hit him. They had not taken anything. One of them had said something once, in the second week, about his shoes.", "That was all. One sentence about his shoes, six weeks ago."], ["“So why?” said Palesa.", "“Because I do not know what happens the second time,” said Thabang."], ["## Eight", "She thought that was the most honest and most sensible thing she had ever heard him say, and she told him so, which surprised him."], ["But she also did the sums with him, on paper, at the kitchen table.", "Eighteen extra minutes a day. Five days a week. Six weeks so far.", "That was nine hours. He had spent nine hours of his life walking away from a sentence about his shoes."], ["## Nine", "They did not solve it in one conversation and Palesa says anybody who tells you otherwise is selling something."], ["What they did was make it smaller. She walked the short way with him for a week, not next to him, but behind, about thirty metres.", "Nothing happened. The boys did not look up. The boys, it turned out, had no idea who he was."], ["## Ten", "That was the thing that undid it in the end.", "Not courage. Information."], ["He had spent six weeks believing he was the subject of something, and the actual situation was that four bored teenagers on a concrete pipe had made one lazy comment and forgotten it inside an hour."], ["## Eleven", "He went back to the short way in the ninth week.", "It was not brave and it did not feel like a victory. He was still slightly sick about it for the first three days and then he was not."], ["Palesa never told their mother, which she has since decided was wrong, and would do differently."], ["## Twelve", "The reason she would do it differently is the nine hours.", "He was managing it. He had a system. The system was costing him nine hours and a low background hum of dread, and he would have carried on paying that indefinitely rather than tell anybody."], ["“That is the bit that frightens me looking back,” she says. “Not the boys. That he had a way of coping, and the way of coping was what kept it invisible.”"]], "wc": 771, "words": [{"w": "route", "m": "the way you go to get somewhere"}, {"w": "concrete", "m": "hard grey building material"}, {"w": "instinct", "m": "a feeling about what to do, without thinking"}, {"w": "eventually", "m": "in the end, after some time"}, {"w": "sensible", "m": "showing good judgement"}, {"w": "honest", "m": "truthful"}, {"w": "courage", "m": "being brave when it is hard"}, {"w": "information", "m": "facts about something"}, {"w": "victory", "m": "a win"}, {"w": "invisible", "m": "cannot be seen"}]}, {"title": "The Braid Money", "blurb": "Lesedi can braid. Six girls in her street want braids. What she has not worked out is what her time is worth.", "n": 7, "pages": [["## One", "Lesedi could braid before she could properly ride a bicycle. Her aunt taught her on a doll and then on her cousin and then, at eleven, on paying customers.", "By thirteen she was good. Not learning. Good."], ["## Two", "The first customer was Naledi from four houses down, who asked on a Saturday morning while Lesedi was hanging washing.", "“How much?”"], ["Lesedi had not thought about it for one second in her life.", "“Fifty,” she said, because it was a number."], ["## Three", "It took four hours and twenty minutes.", "Her fingers hurt. Her back hurt from leaning. Naledi was delighted and told three people, and by the following weekend Lesedi had two more bookings at fifty rand each."], ["## Four", "By the sixth week she was doing three heads a weekend and making a hundred and fifty rand, and she was exhausted.", "It took her until the eighth week to sit down and work out why the money did not feel like enough."], ["## Five", "She did the sums on the back of a school worksheet.", "Fifty rand. Four and a half hours. That came to eleven rand an hour, and she had to buy the extensions herself, which was eighty rand a pack."], ["When she took the extensions off, she was making almost nothing.", "On two of the six heads she had actually lost money, because those girls had thick hair and needed more than one pack."], ["## Six", "Her mother found her doing this and did not rescue her, which Lesedi is grateful for now and was not grateful for then."], ["“So what are you going to do?”", "“Put the price up.”", "“To what?”", "And Lesedi did not have an answer, because the honest answer was <i>I do not know, I have never known, I said fifty because it was a number.</i>"], ["## Seven", "So she went and found out what it actually cost.", "She asked at the salon in the shopping centre. Box braids there were six hundred rand, hair included, and took two women about three hours."], ["She asked her aunt. Her aunt charged four hundred and fifty and did it alone in five hours."], ["## Eight", "Lesedi had been charging fifty.", "The gap was not small. The gap was not even in the same country as small."], ["## Nine", "The hard part was not the arithmetic. She did the arithmetic in one evening."], ["The hard part was that the six customers were girls from her street who she saw every day, and two of them were her friends, and she had told them fifty."], ["She spent a week trying to find a way to raise the price without anybody being upset with her, and there is no such way, and the week was wasted."], ["## Ten", "In the end she wrote it down, because she could not say it out loud without it coming out as an apology."], ["<i>From October: R250, you bring your own hair. R330 if I buy the hair. It takes me four to five hours.</i>"], ["She sent that to all six of them on the same evening, at the same time, so that nobody could say they had been treated differently."], ["## Eleven", "Three said yes immediately.", "One asked for a discount and Lesedi said no, which took her three attempts to type.", "Two never replied, and one of those two was a friend, and that hurt for about a month."], ["## Twelve", "By December she had four regular customers at two hundred and fifty rand.", "That is one thousand rand a month for roughly eighteen hours of work, and she was fourteen."], ["The girl who asked for the discount came back in February and paid full price and never mentioned it."], ["## Thirteen", "The thing she says about it is not about braiding."], ["“I charged fifty rand for four and a half hours because I was asked a question I had never thought about and I did not want to seem greedy. That is the whole reason. It was not a business decision. It was being afraid of a pause in a conversation.”"], ["“I think a lot of people are still charging their fifty rand at forty years old.”"]], "wc": 676, "words": [{"w": "customers", "m": "people who pay for something"}, {"w": "bookings", "m": "appointments made in advance"}, {"w": "exhausted", "m": "extremely tired"}, {"w": "extensions", "m": "added hair used for braiding"}, {"w": "grateful", "m": "thankful"}, {"w": "arithmetic", "m": "working with numbers"}, {"w": "discount", "m": "money taken off the price"}, {"w": "regular", "m": "happening again and again"}, {"w": "decision", "m": "a choice you make"}, {"w": "greedy", "m": "wanting more than your share"}]}, {"title": "Second Language", "blurb": "Tumi has spoken English at school since Grade R. This year a teacher asks her to write in Setswana, and she finds out how much she has lost.", "n": 8, "pages": [["## One", "The task was one page in your home language, for a project about heritage, due in two weeks.", "Tumi looked at it and thought it would take her forty minutes."], ["## Two", "She sat down on the Sunday with a pen and wrote the first sentence and then stopped.", "The sentence was fine. The second sentence was where it went wrong, because she needed a word and the word that came was English, and when she reached for the Setswana one it was not there."], ["Not forgotten. Not misremembered. Simply not there, as though it had never been."], ["## Three", "She sat for an hour and produced four sentences, and two of them were wrong in ways she could feel but could not fix."], ["## Four", "She speaks Setswana. That is the thing.", "She speaks it to her grandmother on the phone every Sunday. She understands every word anybody says to her in it."], ["But she had never written a paragraph in it in her life, and she was twelve, and nobody had ever noticed because nobody had ever asked."], ["## Five", "Her mother read the four sentences.", "She did not laugh, which Tumi had been afraid of, and she did not get upset, which Tumi had been more afraid of."], ["“You write like somebody speaking on the phone,” she said. “Which is exactly what you are.”"], ["## Six", "They worked on it on the Tuesday and Thursday evenings.", "Her mother would say a sentence and Tumi would write it, and then they would look at what she had written and find the places where she had put English grammar into Setswana words."], ["There were many. There were more in the second half of the page than the first, because she got tired and stopped watching for it."], ["## Seven", "The thing that shook her was not the mistakes.", "It was on the Thursday, when her mother used a word she did not know at all, and had to explain it, and the explanation took two minutes."], ["It was an ordinary word. Her mother had used it without thinking. Tumi had never heard it."], ["## Eight", "“How many words do you think there are that I do not have?” she asked."], ["Her mother thought about it honestly, which Tumi appreciated and slightly regretted asking for."], ["“Thousands,” she said. “You have the words a child needs at home. You do not have the words for arguing, or for working, or for anything that happens at a meeting. Those are the ones you would have got at school, if you had gone to school in it.”"], ["## Nine", "Tumi handed in the page and got a good mark, because her mother is a careful person and because the page was, in the end, correct.", "She did not feel good about the mark."], ["## Ten", "What she did afterwards took about a year and nobody made her do it."], ["She started writing her Sunday phone calls down. Not the whole call. Three or four sentences afterwards, of what her grandmother had said, in Setswana, in a notebook.", "At first it took her twenty minutes to write four sentences. By the middle of the year it took five."], ["## Eleven", "Her grandmother found out about the notebook in the December holidays and asked to see it.", "She read four pages of her own words written down by her granddaughter, with mistakes in, over about ten minutes."], ["Then she said something that Tumi wrote down that night and still has.", "“I did not know anybody was keeping these.”"], ["## Twelve", "Tumi is in Grade 11 now and takes Setswana at Home Language level, which she chose herself against advice, because the marks are harder."], ["She is not fluent in the way her mother is. She says she probably never will be, and that this is a real loss and not one anybody should tell her to feel fine about."], ["## Thirteen", "“What nobody explains,” she says, “is that you can lose a language while you are still speaking it. You keep the kitchen words. You lose everything else, slowly, and you do not notice because the kitchen words still work.”"], ["“I noticed because a teacher asked me to write one page. One page. That is all it took to show me.”"]], "wc": 692, "words": [{"w": "heritage", "m": "what is passed down from those before you"}, {"w": "language", "m": "the words a group of people speak"}, {"w": "misremembered", "m": "remembered wrongly"}, {"w": "paragraph", "m": "a group of sentences about one idea"}, {"w": "grammar", "m": "the rules for putting a language together"}, {"w": "ordinary", "m": "normal, nothing special"}, {"w": "explanation", "m": "a reason given for something"}, {"w": "appreciated", "m": "was thankful for"}, {"w": "notebook", "m": "a small book you write in"}, {"w": "fluent", "m": "able to speak a language easily"}]}, {"title": "Against the Motion", "blurb": "Katlego is put on the side of the debate she disagrees with. She has five days to argue for something she thinks is wrong.", "n": 9, "pages": [["## One", "The motion was: <i>This house believes that learners should be allowed to use phones in class.</i>"], ["Katlego had views on this. Katlego had very strong views on this, and they were all on the side of yes."], ["Ms Mahlangu put her on the opposing team."], ["## Two", "“I do not agree with this,” said Katlego, at the front, after everybody else had left.", "“I know.”", "“So why?”", "“Because you are the only person in that class who will be annoyed enough to do it properly.”"], ["## Three", "The first two days were wasted and she knew it while it was happening."], ["She spent them building arguments that a person who agreed with her could knock over, which is a very comfortable thing to do and completely useless."], ["## Four", "On the third day she changed method, because she was getting nowhere.", "She wrote out the best case for phones in class, properly, as strongly as she could, the way she actually believed it."], ["Then she went through it line by line and asked one question of each line: <i>is this true, or do I just want it to be true?</i>"], ["## Five", "Three of her seven points survived.", "The other four were things she had believed for years and had never once looked at."], ["One was that phones help you research in class. When she thought about it honestly, she had never used a phone to research anything in a lesson. Not once. She had used it to message people."], ["## Six", "That was an unpleasant afternoon.", "Finding out that four of your seven reasons are decorations is not a nice thing to find out about yourself, and she wanted to stop and could not."], ["## Seven", "The debate was on the Friday.", "Katlego spoke second for the opposition and she spoke for four minutes."], ["She did not use the obvious arguments about distraction and cheating, because everybody expects those and everybody has an answer ready."], ["## Eight", "She used the three surviving points and she said, at the start, that she had begun the week on the other side.", "The room went quiet in a particular way when she said that."], ["She said the strongest argument for phones was research, and then she said she had gone through her own last month honestly and had not used a phone to research one single thing in a classroom, and asked how many people in the room could say differently."], ["## Nine", "Nobody put a hand up. Not on either team."], ["The opposition won by a distance, and Ms Mahlangu, who was not a person who said things lightly, said afterwards that it was the best schools debate she had judged in eleven years."], ["## Ten", "The part Katlego did not expect came after."], ["She still thinks phones should be allowed in class. She has not changed her mind."], ["But she now holds that view with three reasons instead of seven, and she knows which four she threw away and why, and she says that makes the view stronger rather than weaker."], ["## Eleven", "She has done the same thing with a lot of things since.", "Write down what you believe. Go through it line by line. Ask whether each line is true or just comfortable."], ["She says most people never do it because it is genuinely unpleasant and because nobody makes you."], ["## Twelve", "“Ms Mahlangu did not put me on that side to change my mind,” she says.", "“She put me there because arguing for the thing you already believe teaches you nothing at all. You are just decorating.”"], ["“I found out that four of my reasons were decoration. I am twenty-one and that is still the most useful thing school ever did to me.”"]], "wc": 604, "words": [{"w": "motion", "m": "the statement being debated"}, {"w": "opposing", "m": "on the other side"}, {"w": "arguments", "m": "reasons given to support a view"}, {"w": "useless", "m": "of no use at all"}, {"w": "honestly", "m": "truthfully"}, {"w": "unpleasant", "m": "not nice"}, {"w": "decorations", "m": "things added only to look good"}, {"w": "distraction", "m": "something that takes your attention away"}, {"w": "opposition", "m": "the team arguing against"}, {"w": "genuinely", "m": "really and truly"}]}, {"title": "The Photograph in the Passage", "blurb": "There is a photograph of a man in the passage. Nobody has ever said who he is, and Rethabile has never asked.", "n": 10, "pages": [["## One", "The photograph had been in the passage for Rethabile's whole life, at the height of an adult's eye, in a thin black frame.", "A man in his twenties, in a jacket, not smiling, standing in front of a wall."], ["Nobody had ever said who he was, and she had never asked, and by twelve the not-asking had become a thing in itself."], ["## Two", "There are questions you can ask at four which become impossible by twelve.", "At four you point and say who is that. At twelve you have walked past it eleven thousand times without asking, and asking now means admitting you never knew."], ["## Three", "She asked her cousin first, which was cowardly and which she admits.", "Her cousin did not know either. Her cousin had also never asked. They had a strange ten minutes discovering they had both been doing the same thing for years."], ["## Four", "She asked her mother on a Wednesday in the holidays while they were peeling vegetables, which is where the hard questions get asked in that house."], ["“The photograph in the passage.”", "Her mother carried on peeling.", "“What about it?”", "“Who is it?”"], ["## Five", "There was a long pause, and Rethabile understood in that pause that this was not a small question and that she had picked the right day."], ["“That is my brother,” her mother said.", "Rethabile had not known her mother had a brother."], ["## Six", "His name was Kabelo. He was two years older than her mother.", "He died in 1993, at twenty-four, and the details of how are not the kind of thing this story needs and Rethabile does not tell people."], ["What matters is that he died in 1993, which was the year before everything changed, and that her mother was twenty-two."], ["## Seven", "“Why did nobody tell me?”"], ["Her mother put down the peeler."], ["“Because when you were small I could not say it without crying, and I did not want you frightened. And then you were bigger and it had been so long that saying it would have been strange. And then you were twelve.”"], ["## Eight", "That was the answer, and Rethabile has thought about it many times since, because it is not really an answer about Kabelo at all."], ["It is an answer about how a silence gets built. Nobody decides on it. It happens one reasonable postponement at a time, and then one day it is twelve years old and has its own weight."], ["## Nine", "They talked for about two hours that afternoon and the vegetables were very late.", "Rethabile learned that he had been funny. That he could not sing at all and sang anyway. That he had taught her mother to drive in a car with no handbrake."], ["Small things. Her mother had not said any of them out loud in years and some of them she had to stop in the middle of."], ["## Ten", "Rethabile did one thing after that, and it took her about three months.", "She wrote down everything her mother told her, in a notebook, in her own handwriting, in no particular order."], ["Then she went to her aunt and got more. Then to her grandmother, which was the hardest conversation and the shortest."], ["## Eleven", "The notebook is not long. It is about eleven pages.", "Eleven pages is not much for a person who was twenty-four years old."], ["But it is eleven pages more than there was, and the photograph in the passage is now a photograph of somebody with a name, who could not sing, and who taught his sister to drive in a car with no handbrake."], ["## Twelve", "Her younger brother is nine. He asked about the photograph last year and Rethabile told him in about four minutes, standing in the passage.", "He said, “Oh,” and went off to do something else, and that was that."], ["## Thirteen", "“It cost him nothing,” she says. “Four minutes standing in a passage. It cost me twelve years of walking past a stranger in my own house.”"], ["“The silence was not anybody's fault. That is what I would want people to understand. Nobody decided. It just grew, because every single year it was slightly easier not to say it.”"]], "wc": 685, "words": [{"w": "photograph", "m": "a picture taken with a camera"}, {"w": "passage", "m": "a narrow way through a house"}, {"w": "impossible", "m": "cannot be done"}, {"w": "cowardly", "m": "not brave"}, {"w": "discovering", "m": "finding out"}, {"w": "details", "m": "the small facts about something"}, {"w": "frightened", "m": "afraid"}, {"w": "postponement", "m": "putting something off until later"}, {"w": "handbrake", "m": "the brake that holds a parked car"}, {"w": "handwriting", "m": "the way a person writes by hand"}]}, {"title": "Nineteen Seconds", "blurb": "Ayanda's best time for 100 metres has not moved in seven months. She has been training harder every single week.", "n": 11, "pages": [["## One", "In February she ran the hundred metres in fourteen point two seconds, which was a personal best and which she was extremely pleased about.", "In September she ran fourteen point two seconds."], ["In between she had trained five days a week for seven months."], ["## Two", "Her response to this, for most of those seven months, was to train harder.", "When the time did not move in April, she added a day. When it did not move in June, she added hill sprints."], ["By August she was training six days a week and was tired all the time and her time had not moved by one hundredth of a second."], ["## Three", "The coach at the club was called Mr Adams and he was seventy and he had watched her do this all year without saying much."], ["In September he asked her to bring her training book.", "She did not have a training book."], ["## Four", "“So what do you do on a Tuesday?”", "“Run.”", "“How far?”", "“Until I am tired.”"], ["He did not say anything unkind. He just wrote down what she said, in his own book, and looked at it."], ["## Five", "“You are a sprinter,” he said eventually. “How much of your week is actually sprinting?”"], ["She worked it out standing there and the answer, once she was honest about it, was about ten minutes.", "Ten minutes out of roughly six hours."], ["## Six", "The rest was running. Long, steady, hard, exhausting running, which she had believed was the thing that made you faster because it was the thing that made you tired."], ["“You have trained yourself into an excellent distance runner,” said Mr Adams. “You are competing in an event that lasts fourteen seconds.”"], ["## Seven", "He changed almost everything and most of the changes involved doing less.", "Four days instead of six