 "a runner who races short distances"}, {"w": "exhausting", "m": "making you very tired"}, {"w": "competing", "m": "taking part in a contest"}, {"w": "convinced", "m": "completely sure"}, {"w": "measuring", "m": "finding out the size or amount"}, {"w": "progress", "m": "moving forward, improving"}]}, {"title": "The Thing About Marks", "blurb": "Onthatile gets 89 percent and is not allowed to be happy about it. It takes her until Grade 11 to work out what that did.", "n": 12, "pages": [["## One", "The mark was eighty-nine percent for the June maths paper, which was the highest mark in her grade.", "She brought it home folded in her bag and unfolded it on the kitchen table."], ["Her father looked at it for a while.", "“Where are the other eleven?”"], ["## Two", "He was not being cruel. This is important and Onthatile did not understand it until much later.", "He had left school at sixteen. He had done sums on the back of delivery slips for twenty years. He genuinely wanted to know which parts she had got wrong so that she would not get them wrong again."], ["But she was twelve, and what she heard was: <i>eighty-nine is not enough.</i>"], ["## Three", "So she chased the other eleven.", "In September she got ninety-four. The question was the same. Where are the other six."], ["## Four", "By Grade 9 she had a system.", "She would work out, before any test, what the highest possible mark was, and then how close she could get to it, and then she would go and get that."], ["It worked. Her marks were extremely good. She was the kind of learner teachers used as an example."], ["## Five", "The thing that went wrong went wrong very slowly and nobody noticed, including her."], ["She stopped choosing anything she might be bad at.", "She dropped art after Grade 8, which she had loved, because her marks in it were in the seventies and she could not make them move."], ["## Six", "She did not join the debating team, although she wanted to, because she had watched one and had realised